1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:03,985
Checkout is back. But one customer still needs help.

2
00:00:04,085 --> 00:00:07,131
That's the gap I built RelayCart to close.

3
00:00:08,300 --> 00:00:12,412
When checkout fails, engineering must restore the service.

4
00:00:12,512 --> 00:00:16,667
Support still needs to know which customers can actually buy again.

5
00:00:17,467 --> 00:00:21,152
This release deploys successfully, but checkout fails.

6
00:00:21,252 --> 00:00:25,748
RelayCart catches that business failure and opens one incident.

7
00:00:26,567 --> 00:00:29,143
I made rollback a human decision.

8
00:00:29,243 --> 00:00:36,192
Approval is tied to the exact release change, so a stale approval cannot overwrite a
newer version.

9
00:00:37,567 --> 00:00:39,673
The operator approves once.

10
00:00:39,773 --> 00:00:44,120
The system rolls back, then checks whether checkout actually works.

11
00:00:44,933 --> 00:00:47,189
A green health check isn't enough.

12
00:00:47,289 --> 00:00:53,791
RelayCart also creates a checkout and reads the order back before calling the
service recovered.

13
00:00:54,933 --> 00:00:59,963
Next, eighteen affected customers get linked cases in GoHighLevel.

14
00:01:00,063 --> 00:01:02,489
Support can see who needs a response.

15
00:01:03,300 --> 00:01:07,177
Acme confirms checkout works. Ocean still needs help.

16
00:01:07,277 --> 00:01:12,925
Customer outcomes stay separate, so recovery cannot close an unresolved case.

17
00:01:13,733 --> 00:01:16,779
Ocean's reply creates one support task.

18
00:01:16,879 --> 00:01:21,396
Unanswered cases stay open. Silence never counts as success.

19
00:01:22,200 --> 00:01:26,269
Workflows coordinate recovery. Postgres preserves state.

20
00:01:26,369 --> 00:01:30,951
AI advice is validated before it reaches the workflow.

21
00:01:31,767 --> 00:01:38,332
I designed for human authorization, verified checkout, and durable customer follow
up.

22
00:01:38,432 --> 00:01:40,880
The recorded run shows all three.

23
00:01:41,767 --> 00:01:46,668
I'm Kabir. I build reliable automation around customer outcomes.

24
00:01:46,768 --> 00:01:49,216
Explore the project on GitHub.
